  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nA seasonably strong and expansive high-pressure system over the\nGreat Plains continues to build eastward into the Midwest this \nafternoon, following a surface low moving into New England. \nStrong northerly winds between these systems are pushing the \nseason's coldest airmass southward into the area, resulting in a\ncloudy, chilly, and breezy day across the region. A few widely \nscattered showers remain possible across northern Illinois \nthrough sunset as an upper-level trough moves overhead. However,\nshower activity will primarily concentrate over Lake Michigan \nlate this afternoon and evening as lake-effect parameters \nrapidly improve. This should lead to focused lake-effect \nshowers\u2014and potentially a few thunderstorms\u2014shifting inland from\nsouthern Lake Michigan into northwestern Indiana this evening. \nScattered lake effect showers may linger after midnight, but \nthey are expected to diminish toward Saturday morning as \ninversion heights lower and lake-effect parameters weaken \nfollowing the passage of the upper-level trough. \n\nOutside of lake-effect precipitation and cloud cover in \nnorthwestern Indiana, skies will clear and winds will gradually \nsubside this evening. This will be especially true across \ninterior portions of northern Illinois, where the coldest \ntemperatures of the season are forecast tonight. The current \nforecast remains on track for a couple of hours of near-freezing\ntemperatures early Saturday morning, most notably across far \nnorthern Illinois from the Fox Valley westward. Consequently, no\nchanges were made to the frost and freeze headlines in effect \nfor interior northern Illinois late tonight. \n\nFollowing a chilly and frosty start on Saturday, full sunshine \nand light winds are forecast as the surface high moves directly \nover the region. The abundant sunshine will help temperatures \nrebound into the upper 50s during the afternoon. Another chilly \nnight will follow Saturday night as temperatures drop back into \nthe 30s. Given the light winds, additional frosty conditions are\npossible, particularly east of I-55 where the lightest winds \nare expected. We will continue to monitor this period for \npotential frost headlines. \n\nThe cold snap will be short-lived as conditions transition back\nto a mild and dry autumn pattern. High temperatures are \nforecast to return to the 60s on Sunday and Monday, before \nwarming into the 70s for the remainder of next week with little \nto no chance of rain.\n\nKJB",
